<section id="how">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="eyebrow display"><span class="chap">02</span> How work flows</div>
    <h1 class="display doc-title">The mental model in one breath</h1>
    <p class="lede-sm">You've run pipelines like this with humans in every box. The difference
    to look for: every hand-off is a record, and nothing passes a gate on vibes.</p>
    <p>Every repo is a <b>hive</b> — its own beads database, served by <code>bd</code>'s shared
    local Dolt server and kept out of your tracked tree under <code>.beads/</code> until you
    furnish the hive. Issues carry a short, stable <b>prefix</b>
    (<code>acme-api-1</code>) derived from the stable part of identity (org + repo); the parts
    that can change (provider, org) live in <b>labels</b>. Issue history publishes to
    <code>refs/dolt/data</code> on the repo's <em>own git remote</em> — a separate ref namespace
    that never touches branches or PRs, so there is <b>nothing hosted to run</b>. A workspace-level
    aggregate (the <b>hub</b>) pulls every hive into one read view, so you can ask "what's ready
    to work on anywhere?"; <b>HQ</b> is the separate, authoritative fleet store.</p>
    <p><code>bh</code> is deliberately a thin orchestrator: <code>bd</code> (beads) owns issues,
    <code>git</code> owns history, <code>git-workspace</code> owns cloning. What <code>bh</code>
    adds is the process — <code>bh work</code> drives a bead through its lifecycle so agents never
    improvise raw <code>git</code> / <code>gh pr</code> for the lifecycle itself.</p>

    <h3 class="display" id="how-tenets">The tenets</h3>
    <div class="cardgrid">
      <div class="card"><h4><span class="hexdot">⬢</span>Planes, kept separate</h4>
        <p><b>Control</b> governs the factory. <b>Planning</b> turns ideas into
        molecules. <b>Development</b> — the CLI still calls it <em>Integration</em> — executes
        them. <b>Release</b> proves a version and publishes it. Each plane has its own verbs and seats;
        they hand off sequentially and never step into each other's role. <b>Assurance</b> is
        the one exception — a cross-cutting gate layer, not a sequential plane.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h4><span class="hexdot">⬢</span>Merging is not releasing</h4>
        <p>Development is high-frequency and dirty: each bead gets its own worktree and lands on
        an <b>always-green</b> line. Release is a separate, deliberate, gated act — preflight,
        bump, attest, await, push — <a href="#release-bump">described below</a>.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h4><span class="hexdot">⬢</span>Lossless history</h4>
        <p>Agents do the merging, so history is audited: merge <code>--no-ff</code> at the
        boundary, <b>never squash there</b>. Workers squash only their own local checkpoints
        into clean conventional digests <em>before</em> submit (tiered retention).</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h4><span class="hexdot">⬢</span>Unit of work = a bead</h4>
        <p>Worktree → implement → refine → check → submit → review → merge. One bead, one
        branch (<code>wt/bead/issue/&lt;id&gt;</code>), one reviewable change.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
<section id="ring">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="eyebrow display">The ring</div>
    <h2 class="display">Six phases, one ring — and one edge nobody closes</h2>
    <p>A car factory isn't successful because the car left the line — it's successful if the
    car survives the road. Software is no different: what happens in production is the only
    signal that says whether the last decision was any good, so it has to come back round to
    the next one.</p>
    <LoopHex />
  </div>
</section>
<section id="planes-strip">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="eyebrow display">The four planes</div>
    <h2 class="display">Ideation → Planning → Development → Release</h2>
    <p class="lede-sm">Control governs the sequence; Assurance gates across it. Each plane is a
    state machine with named re-entry doors — <a href="/planes/">the full map</a> shows every
    state. This is the skeleton.</p>
    <div class="planestrip" role="list">
      <div class="planestrip-control"><span>CONTROL</span> supervisor · director · custodian · controller</div>
      <a role="listitem" class="planestrip-plane is-direction" href="/planes/#ideation">
        <b>Ideation</b><small>report → triage → adopt</small>
        <span class="status direction">intake ships · plane is direction</span></a>
      <a role="listitem" class="planestrip-plane" href="/planes/#planning">
        <b>Planning</b><small>idea → gated molecule</small>
        <span class="status shipped">shipped</span></a>
      <a role="listitem" class="planestrip-plane" href="/planes/#development">
        <b>Development</b><small>bead → merged · CLI: integration</small>
        <span class="status shipped">shipped</span></a>
      <a role="listitem" class="planestrip-plane" href="/planes/#release">
        <b>Release</b><small>attested tree → atomic push</small>
        <span class="status shipped">verbs shipped · seat roadmap</span></a>
      <div class="planestrip-assure"><span>ASSURANCE</span> security gate, parallel to review, warden-only · <em>Observation and Feedback: named, not specified</em></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
