    <div class="wrap" style="padding:0">
      <h3 class="display" id="pipeline-green">What keeps the line green</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Serialized merges.</b> Merge is a separate seat (the merger) gated by
        <code>bd merge-slot</code> — parallel developers, one merge at a time.</li>
        <li><b>The conflict ladder.</b> <code>clean → rebased → union → bounce</code>: try a
        clean <code>--no-ff</code>, then rebase-and-retry, then path-whitelisted union merge
        (append-only files only, re-validated), else bounce with the branch restored. No work
        is ever lost.</li>
        <li><b>Validation modes.</b> Every bead validates in isolation at submit;
        <code>relaxed</code> / <code>conservative</code> / <code>loose</code> tune how often the
        <em>combination</em> is re-tested. If a re-validation fails on a safe-to-rewrite branch,
        the tip is rolled back before anyone can observe it; a shared branch escalates for a
        forward fix instead.</li>
        <li><b>Evidence is keyed to the tree.</b> A green result is stored against the exact
        tree hash and command that produced it. A fresh exact match may be reused where the caller
        allows it; a graph-derived <em>attest key</em> may carry across a change only when its whole
        input closure is unchanged. Unknown impact fails closed to the full run, and a reused key
        is never a claim that the whole tree is green — <a href="/planes/#attestation">how reuse
        works</a>.</li>
        <li><b>The batch exception.</b> Cohesive linear chains can run as one shared
        <code>wt/batch/&lt;group&gt;</code> worktree, merged once — guarded by cohesion, a size
        cap of 5, no conflicting model preferences, one review gate. A batch fails and bounces as a unit; per-bead
        commits stay bisectable inside the bubble.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="note"><b>Where the ideas come from.</b> <span class="status direction">direction</span>
      The pipeline starts at <em>ideate</em>, which quietly assumes someone showed up with an
      idea — capping the whole factory at one person's imagination on a given afternoon. What
      ships upstream of planning today is <a href="/fleet/#hq-intake">intake and triage</a>.
      The ideation plane is meant to add a loop that doesn't wait for someone to show up: read the repo's own vision and strategy documents, derive candidate
      topics from how those documents are actually structured rather than from a generic
      checklist, then generate against each topic in parallel — deliberately blind to the
      others, so the batch stays divergent instead of converging on the same three obvious
      ideas. Survivors are deduplicated, judged against the vision, and filed through
      <code>bh plan file</code> like any other molecule, so nothing skips the kickoff gate. Its
      states and seat are not defined yet.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="release">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="eyebrow display">Merge order</div>
    <h2 class="display">Merge order is planned, not first-come-first-served</h2>
    <p>Landing approved beads in whatever order they clear review is fine until a molecule mixes
    <em>kinds</em> of change — a hotfix for the released version, additive features that should
    ship as one clean minor bump, and a breaking change that must not leak into a patch. Review
    order produces a history nobody can version cleanly. So a bead can carry its semantic-version
    impact, and the merge sequence is computed from it:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>release:</code> — <code>breaking</code>, <code>feature</code> or <code>fix</code>.
      A fixed vocabulary, identical in every hive, so the ordering means the same thing
      everywhere.</li>
      <li><code>wave:</code> — a free-form label grouping additive features that should land
      together as one bump. Distinct from the worktree batching label.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The default strategy, <em>stable-versioning</em>, orders in four tiers: fixes for the
    released version flush first, up to a churn budget; then additive features, grouped by wave;
    then any fixes past that budget; then breaking changes last, so they never leak into a patch
    or minor window early. Both knobs are per-hive configuration
    (<code>release.strategy</code>, <code>release.fix_churn_budget</code>).</p>
<pre><code>bh release order   # the strategy-preferred merge sequence, read-only</code></pre>
    <p>It is <b>advisory</b>. It decides sequence and nothing else — it never claims, merges or
    mutates anything, and the scheduler consults the same scorer so dispatch and merge never
    disagree about the order. The one hard counterpart is opt-in per hive: with
    <code>release.enforce_hold</code> on, a <code>release:breaking</code> bead gets a
    <code>release-hold:</code> gate that blocks its merge until a releaser resolves it — and only
    a releaser can.</p>

    <h3 class="display" id="release-bump">Cutting the version: the guarded bump <span class="status shipped">shipped</span></h3>
    <p>Ordering decides what lands; the bump decides what ships. The rule that makes it safe:
    the release gate runs against the <em>exact tree the bump creates</em>, not the tree that was
    green before it.</p>
    <ol class="bumpflow" aria-label="The guarded release bump">
      <li><b>preflight</b><span>requires green proof for the current tree before anything moves</span></li>
      <li><b>bump</b><span><code>cz bump</code> writes version metadata and a local tag — a new, unproven tree</span></li>
      <li><b>attest</b><span>runs the <code>push-main</code> gate on that exact tree</span></li>
      <li><b>await</b><span>blocks until the verdict; red refuses, green permits</span></li>
      <li class="is-green"><b>push</b><span>one atomic push moves <code>main</code> and the tag together</span></li>
    </ol>
<pre><code>bh release preflight   # green proof for the current tree, or refuse
cz bump                # new tree + local tag — unproven
bh release attest      # the push-main gate against that exact tree
bh release await       # block until GREEN, then push main + tag atomically
bh release recover     # after a failed push: measure the remote before deciding</code></pre>
    <p>The tag reaching the remote is the point of no return. Before it, recovery can undo
    locally; after it, roll forward — published history is never rewritten.</p>
    <div class="note"><b>Verbs ship; the seat is roadmap.</b> <span class="status direction">direction</span>
    The commands above run today. The <em>releaser</em> seat that owns them in the org chart —
    changelog authorship, the release plane as a staffed stage — is still marked roadmap in the
    reference docs, so a human runs the bump.</div>
  </div>
</section>

